======================================================================================================================
PART 3 -- the decisive measurement: what has to be measured, and how well
======================================================================================================================
  The deep-MOND limit of the framework's own a0-line is v^4 = G M_b a0 EXACTLY, so a rotation-dominated
  system with g_bar << a0 measures a0 directly:   a0 = v_flat^4 / (G M_b).
  Hence  log10 a0 = 4 log10 v_flat - log10 M_b - log10 G,  and the error budget is FORCED:
         sigma(log10 a0)^2 = (4 sigma_log v)^2 + (sigma_log Mb)^2 .
  [PASS] D3-a  REGRESSION: separating FLAT (0.00) from LCDM-native (+0.328) at 20:1 odds (log10 B = 1.30) needs a TOTAL uncertainty of 0.134 dex on ONE deep-MOND point.  This reproduces the repository's registered 0.13 dex from the Gaussian two-hypothesis Bayes factor, derived not copied   (sigma needed = 0.1341 dex (registered 0.13-0.134))
     sigma_v/v  4 sigma_logv |  sigma_logMb |  total dex | 20:1 at 0.134?
          0.03        0.0521 |         0.05 |     0.0722 | YES
          0.03        0.0521 |         0.10 |     0.1128 | YES
          0.03        0.0521 |         0.15 |     0.1588 | no
          0.05        0.0869 |         0.05 |     0.1002 | YES
          0.05        0.0869 |         0.10 |     0.1325 | YES
          0.05        0.0869 |         0.15 |     0.1733 | no
          0.08        0.1390 |         0.05 |     0.1477 | no
          0.08        0.1390 |         0.10 |     0.1712 | no
          0.08        0.1390 |         0.15 |     0.2045 | no
          0.10        0.1737 |         0.05 |     0.1808 | no
          0.10        0.1737 |         0.10 |     0.2004 | no
          0.10        0.1737 |         0.15 |     0.2295 | no
  [PASS] D3-b  the budget CLOSES, but only just: 5% rotation velocities and 0.10 dex baryonic mass give 0.132 dex, at the 20:1 threshold.  The BARYONIC MASS, not the velocity, is the binding constraint -- 4x the velocity error enters, so 5% on v is already 0.087 dex and leaves almost nothing for M_b   (5% on v + 0.10 dex on M_b = 0.1325 dex vs 0.134 needed)
  [PASS] D3-c  ...and if the framework's own DESI-CPL band (-0.122 dex low) is honoured rather than the single 0.00, the separation GROWS to 0.450 dex and the requirement RELAXES to 0.184 dex.  Reported because it cuts in the framework's favour and should not be quietly omitted   (separation 0.450 dex -> sigma needed 0.184 dex)
  
  THE MEASUREMENT, stated concretely:  ONE strongly lensed, rotation-dominated galaxy at 2.3 < z < 2.9,
  selected to have g_bar(R_out) < 0.3 a0 on BOTH footings (so the deep-MOND limit is actually reached);
  rotation from JWST/NIRSpec IFU (G235H/F170LP catches Halpha and [OIII] together over 2.32 < z < 3.83);
  an INDEPENDENT gas mass from ALMA CO(3-2) in Band 3 (1.98 < z < 3.12) so M_b does not lean on a
  star-formation-rate calibration.  Required: dv/v < 5%, d log10 M_b < 0.10 dex.  N = 1 suffices at 20:1;
  this is a targeted observation, not a survey.

======================================================================================================================
PART 4 -- THE MEASUREMENT THAT DISAGREES: MUSE-DARK III.  Reported honestly, both ways.
======================================================================================================================
  Ciocan et al. fit a0(z) = a0(0) + a1 z over 0.33 < z < 1.44: a0(0) = 1.0 +- 0.04, a1 = +1.59 +- 0.105 (units 1e-10 m/s^2 per z).
  To compare a LAW with a LINEAR FIT, each law is projected onto the same linear slope over the same
  redshift window by least squares -- the only apples-to-apples comparison available without the data.
    hypothesis                              effective a1 |  sigma vs MUSE (raw) |  after drift folding f=0.5
    BRANCH A (flat / stage-17)                    -0.000 |                15.14 |                       1.91
    BRANCH A1 (DESI CPL, declining)               -0.023 |                15.36 |                       1.97
    BRANCH B (a0 propto H)                         0.997 |                 5.65 |                       0.50
    LCDM-native halo drift alone                   0.800 |                 7.52 |                       0.02
  [PASS] D4-a  REGRESSION on the repository's banked MUSE numbers: BRANCH A (flat) is 15.1 sigma from the MUSE slope at face value, and 1.91-3.02 sigma after folding the LCDM assembly drift at f = 0.5-0.3.  Reproduces the banked 15.1 sigma raw and 1.9-3.0 sigma folded   (raw 15.14 sigma (banked 15.14); folded f=0.5 1.91 (banked 1.91), f=0.3 3.02 (banked 3.02))
  [PASS] D4-b  *** AND THE SAME DATUM DISFAVOURS BRANCH B TOO, WHICH THE RECORD DOES NOT STATE. ***  MUSE's rise is FASTER than H(z): the effective a1 for a0 propto H over the MUSE window is 1.00 against the measured 1.59 +- 0.105, i.e. 5.6 sigma.  Ciocan et al. say so in words ('faster than that of H(z)'); it is quantified here.  MUSE is not a datum that rescues the rising branch   (branch B effective a1 = 0.997 vs measured 1.59 +- 0.105  ->  5.65 sigma)
  [PASS] D4-c  THE DOUBLE-COUNTING HAZARD, stated rather than exploited: the drift folding that softens branch A from 15.1 to 1.9 sigma uses an LCDM halo-assembly drift of +0.80/z -- which is essentially the SAME physical effect as branch B's own a0 propto H (effective 1.00/z).  Folding it and then also claiming branch B as a prediction would count one effect twice.  The folded number is therefore a CEILING on the relief branch A may claim, not a result   (assembly drift 0.80/z vs branch B's own 1.00/z -- the same order, so they are not independent)
  [PASS] D4-d  THE FALSIFICATION CONDITION, stated in advance and in the framework's own terms: the stage-17 derived law predicts an effective a1 of -1.32e-06 over the MUSE window -- flat to 5 decimal places.  ANY robust nonzero a1, EITHER SIGN, at >3 sigma after a homogeneous drift-modelled pipeline kills it.  The present status is 1.9-3.0 sigma depending on how much of the drift is credited: NOT a falsification today, and the sharpest live threat the framework has   (predicted a1 = -1.32e-06; measured 1.59 +- 0.105; folded tension 1.91-3.02 sigma)

======================================================================================================================
PART 5 -- both footings, and the one-line summary of what decides it
======================================================================================================================
  canonical : a0(0) = 9.3619e-11;  branch A predicts 9.3619e-11 at EVERY z <= 5;  branch B predicts 3.5299e-10 at z = 2.5
  alt       : a0(0) = 1.1279e-10;  branch A predicts 1.1279e-10 at EVERY z <= 5;  branch B predicts 4.2528e-10 at z = 2.5
  [PASS] D5-a  the a0(z) predictions are footing-independent as RATIOS (the footings differ by a constant 20.5%%, which is a zero-point not a slope), so every dex number above is unchanged by the choice of footing   (alt/canonical = 1.2048 at all z on both branches -> cancels in every ratio)

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT (fbD4)
======================================================================================================================

  THE PREDICTION.  On the framework's own derivation (branch A) a0 is FLAT: |log10 a0(z)/a0(0)| < 1e-3 dex
  for all z <= 3.5 on both the locked and the stage-17 laws.  That flatness is the distinctive content --
  LCDM's emergent halo scale instead RISES by +0.328 dex by z = 2.5 (Dutton-Maccio 2014; +0.448 with Duffy
  2008), and branch B rises by +0.576 dex.  One measurement separates all three.
  Honesty amendment: once DESI-CPL dark energy is allowed the framework's own z = 2.5 prediction is a BAND,
  -0.122 to 0.000 dex, not the single value 0.00 that the registered test uses.

  THE SHARPEST TEST.  a0 = v_flat^4/(G M_b) on ONE strongly lensed, rotation-dominated galaxy at 2.3 < z < 2.9
  selected at g_bar < 0.3 a0, with JWST/NIRSpec rotation and an independent ALMA CO gas mass.  Required total
  uncertainty 0.134 dex for 20:1 odds (0.184 dex if the framework's own CPL band is honoured).  The budget
  closes only just: 5% on v is already 0.087 dex because the velocity enters to the FOURTH power, leaving
  ~0.10 dex for the baryonic mass.  M_b, not kinematics, is the binding constraint.

  THE MEASUREMENT THAT DISAGREES, AND IT IS REAL.  MUSE-DARK III measures a1 = +1.59 +- 0.105, i.e. a0 RISING.
  Against branch A's predicted -1.3e-06 that is 15.1 SIGMA at face value.  Folding the LCDM halo-assembly
  drift (+0.8/z, Magneticum) at 50-70% credit brings it to 1.9-3.0 sigma -- but that folding is close to
  circular, because the drift being folded IS branch B's own effect, so 1.9-3.0 sigma is a CEILING on the
  relief, not a resolution.  Status: NOT a falsification today, and the sharpest live threat on the board.
  A homogeneous, drift-modelled re-analysis at >3 sigma either sign KILLS the flat law outright.
  AND THE SAME DATUM DISFAVOURS BRANCH B at 5.6 sigma, because the measured rise is FASTER than H(z).
  No branch of this framework is supported by the one direct a0(z) measurement in hand.

